unger than the bird fossils, then the argument holds. So, the argument assumes that no such older fossils exist that haven't been found yet. That makes sense as an assumption.\n\nD) It couldn't have been the case that some birds are from one dinosaur and others from another. This is about whether multiple dinosaur lineages gave rise to birds. The argument doesn't directly address this; it's more about the timing of the fossils. So D might not be the main assumption.\n\nWait, let me think again. The argument is that because bird fossils are older than the earliest bird-like dinosaur fossils, the idea that birds are descendants of those dinosaurs is incorrect. So, the argument assumes that the oldest birdlike dinosaur fossils are the ones that are actually the ancestors of the birds. If there were older birdlike fossils, that would support the idea, so the argument assumes that no such older fossils exist. That points to option C.\n\nSo, the correct assumption is that there are no older birdlike dinosaur fossils that haven't been found yet. Therefore, the argument relies on option C.\n</think>\n\nThe argument assumes that the oldest birdlike dinosaur fossils are the correct ancestors of birds, so there are no older ones yet undiscovered. This is necessary to support the conclusion that birds are not descendants of those dinosaurs.\n\nThe correct option is: C",
