      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out the answer to this question based on the given context. Let me read through the context again to make sure I understand it correctly.\n\nThe context says that a lot of people spit everywhere without any regard for consequences because the previous punishment measures aren't serving as a warning. It also mentions that these spitters don't feel shame when they spit or after being punished. So, the main issue seems to be that the current punishments aren't effective in deterring spitting because they don't touch the root of the problem, which is the lack of shame or sense of guilt.\n\nNow, the question is asking which option the author thinks is the best way to punish people who spit everywhere. The options are A, B, C, D.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nA) We should give them proper guidance. This suggests that instead of punishment, offering help or advice might be better. But the context doesn't mention anything about guidance being effective. It talks about punishment not working because people aren't punished enough.\n\nB) Touch the depths of their heart. This seems a bit abstract. It might mean addressing their inner feelings or emotions, but I'm not sure how practical that is in terms of punishment measures.\n\nC) Not specified. This would mean that the author doesn't provide an answer in the context.\n\nD) We need to specify the punishment measures. This suggests that the author believes the current measures aren't enough and that more specific or harsher penalties should be implemented.\n\nFrom the context, the author seems to argue that the previous punishments aren't effective because they don't address the deeper issue, which is the lack of shame. So, the best punishment would likely involve more severe measures that actually touch the heart of the problem. Therefore, option D seems to align with this reasoning because it's about specifying the punishment, implying that the current measures aren't sufficient and more should be done.\n\nI don't think A is the right choice because the context doesn't support the idea that guidance is more effective than punishment. Option B is too vague and doesn't directly address the issue of punishment. Option C is incorrect because the author does provide an answer, which is option D.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: D\n\nThe author argues that previous punishment measures are ineffective because they don't address the root cause of the problem, which is the lack of shame. Therefore, the best approach is to specify more severe punishment measures to deter spitting.\n\nThe correct option is: D",
