ree are in one each. But wait, the problem states that one member is in all three sub-committees, which is three slots. So, that's one member contributing three slots, and the remaining two overlaps (since 9 - 6 = 3, but one member is already using three, so the remaining two overlaps must come from two members each in two sub-committees). So, in total, one member is in three sub-committees, two members are in two sub-committees each, and the remaining three members are in one sub-committee each.\n\nWait, that adds up: 3 (from the triple member) + 2*2 (from two double members) + 3*1 (from three single members) = 3 + 4 + 3 = 10, which is more than 9. Hmm, that can't be right. Maybe I'm miscalculating.\n\nWait, perhaps the correct way is: Each of the three sub-committees has three members, so 3*3=9. We have six members, so the number of overlaps is 9 - 6 = 3. That means three members are in two sub-committees each, because each such member adds one extra slot. So, three members in two sub-committees each account for 3 extra slots, making the total 6 + 3 = 9. So, that makes sense: one member is in all three sub-committees (contributing three slots), and three other members are each in two sub-committees, contributing two each, but wait, that would be 3 (from the triple) + 3*2 (from the three double) = 3 + 6 = 9. But wait, that would require three members each in two sub-committees, but we only have six members in total. Let me see:\n\n- One member is in three sub-committees: 3 slots.\n- Three members are in two sub-committees each: 3*2 = 6 slots.\n- The remaining two members are in one sub-committee each: 2 slots.\nTotal slots: 3 + 6 + 2 = 11, which is more than 9. That's not possible. So, I must have made a mistake.\n\nWait, perhaps the correct breakdown is:\n\n- One member is in three sub-committees: 3 slots.\n- Two members are in two sub-committees each: 2*2 = 4 slots.\n- The remaining three members are in one sub-committee each: 3 slots.\nTotal slots: 3 + 4 + 3 = 1