sub-committees are double members, and the two in one are single.\n\nNow, considering the options:\n\nOption C: P or M is a member of only one sub-committee.\n\nSo, if P or M is one of the two single members, then they are in only one. But it's possible that P or M is one of the double members, meaning they are in two sub-committees. So, is it necessary that at least one of P or M is in only one?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the constraints force P or M to be in only one.\n\nWait, let's think about the constraints again. F and G can't be together, H and I can't be together.\n\nSuppose the person in three sub-committees is, say, M. Then, M is in all three. Now, F and G can't be together, so they must be in different sub-committees. Similarly, H and I can't be together.\n\nIf M is in all three, then each sub-committee must include M and two others. So, each sub-committee has M plus two others. Since F and G can't be together, they must be in different sub-committees. Similarly, H and I must be in different sub-committees.\n\nSo, let's try to assign members to sub-committees.\n\nSub-committee 1: M, F, H\n\nSub-committee 2: M, G, I\n\nSub-committee 3: M, ?, ?\n\nWait, but we have six members: F, G, H, I, M, P.\n\n",
