perhaps B is not correct because it's only talking about the top point, not any point.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the question is considering that the top of the stratosphere is a single point, so B is correct because it's comparing that single point to the point directly below it in the troposphere.\n\nI think I'm overcomplicating this. Let me try to approach it differently.\n\nThe key point is that the stratosphere warms with altitude. So any higher point in the stratosphere is warmer than any lower point. Therefore, the temperature at any point in the stratosphere is higher than the point directly below it in the troposphere. So B is correct.\n\nBut the question is asking which must be true. So both B and D seem correct, but perhaps only D is correct because B's wording is ambiguous.\n\nWait, no, the question is about the top of the stratosphere. So the top of the stratosphere is a specific point, so B is correct because it's stating that at that top point, the temperature is at least as high as the point directly below it in the troposphere.\n\nBut the way B is phrased is a bit confusing. It says \"at any point at the top of the stratosphere,\" which might mean any point in the stratosphere, but I think it's more likely that it's referring to the top point. So perhaps B is correct.\n\nWait, but the top of the stratosphere is a single point, so it's not any point. So B is not correct because it's only talking about the top point, not any point in the stratosphere. Therefore, B is not necessarily true.\n\nSo the correct answer is D because the starting point of the stratosphere is warmer over the poles, so the top of the stratosphere over the poles is warmer than over the equator.\n\nWait, but I'm still confused because I think B is correct because the stratosphere warms with altitude, so any higher point is warmer than the point below it. So B is correct.\n\nI think I'm getting stuck here. Let me try to summarize.\n\n- The troposphere's top temperature is -50\u00b0C