      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out which option supports the meteorologist's argument. The meteorologist says that the number of tornadoes reported annually has more than doubled since the 1950s, but their actual number hasn't increased. They argue that our ability to find tornadoes has improved, so we're just finding a higher percentage now.\n\nHmm, so the key points here are that the reported numbers have gone up, but the actual number hasn't. The meteorologist attributes this to better detection methods. So, the question is asking which option, if true, would most support this argument.\n\nLet me look at the options:\n\nA) The annual number of deaths due to tornadoes has increased steadily since the 1950s.\nB) The number of tornadoes hitting major population centers annually has more than doubled since the 1950s.\nC) The geographic range in which tornadoes are most prevalent has remained roughly constant since the 1950s.\nD) The number of large and medium-sized tornadoes reported annually has remained roughly constant since the 1950s.\n\nI need to find which of these, if true, would support the idea that the actual number of tornadoes hasn't increased, but the reported number has because of better detection.\n\nOption A talks about deaths increasing. If deaths have increased, that could mean more tornadoes are happening, but the meteorologist is saying the actual number hasn't increased. So maybe this doesn't directly support the argument because it's about deaths, not the number detected. Plus, even if deaths increased, it could be due to more severe tornadoes, not necessarily more tornadoes overall.\n\nOption B says the number hitting major population centers has doubled. If the number hitting major areas has doubled, that might suggest that the actual number has increased, which contradicts the meteorologist's point. So this might not support the argument.\n\nOption C states that the geographic range hasn't changed. If tornadoes are still happening in the same places, but we're detecting more, maybe because of better instruments or methods. But this doesn't directly explain why the number of reported tornadoes has gone up while the actual number hasn't. It's more about where they're occurring, not the detection rate.\n\nOption D says the number of large and medium-sized tornadoes hasn't changed. If the size hasn't changed, but the number of reported ones has, that could mean that smaller tornadoes are now being detected, which were previously missed. So, the actual number of large and medium ones hasn't increased, but we're finding more small ones, leading to an overall increase in reported tornadoes. This would support the meteorologist's argument because it shows that the detection methods have improved, allowing us to find more tornadoes, even if the actual number hasn't gone up.\n\nWait, but the meteorologist's point is about the actual number not increasing, but the reported number has. So if the number of large and medium-sized tornadoes hasn't changed, but we're detecting more of the smaller ones, that would mean the total number of reported tornadoes has gone up, but the actual number (large and medium) hasn't. That makes sense because the actual number refers to the true count, and the detection methods have improved to find more of the smaller ones.\n\nSo, Option D seems to support the argument because it shows that the actual number of large and medium tornadoes hasn't increased, but the detection methods have allowed us to find more, leading to an increase in reported numbers.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is D.\n\nThe meteorologist's argument hinges on the idea that the actual number of tornadoes hasn't increased, but the reported number has gone up due to improved detection methods. Option D supports this by indicating that the number of large and medium-sized tornadoes hasn't changed, suggesting that the increase in reported tornadoes is due to detecting more smaller tornadoes, which were previously undetected. This aligns with the meteorologist's reasoning about detection improvements.\n\nThe correct option is: D",
