If the father is asking for reimbursement, maybe Zhang didn't inform the insurance company about his condition. But the option doesn't mention whether Zhang declared having a congenital disease. If he didn't inform the insurance company, that would be a violation because he should have disclosed his health issues. However, the option doesn't specify whether he did inform or not. It just says the father asked for reimbursement, which might imply that the insurance company didn't know about the disease. So, if Zhang didn't inform the insurance company, that's a problem.\n\nOption D: Li was hospitalized with gastric cancer, discharged, and went to work normally. Then, someone introduced him to simple life insurance, and he didn't declare his cancer. So, when filling out the form, he didn't tell the insurance company about his cancer. That's a clear violation because he should have disclosed all relevant health information. Not declaring cancer when it's known would be dishonest and a breach of good faith.\n\nNow, let me analyze each option again to see which one definitely violates the principle.\n\nOption A: Truthful information about health status. No issue here.\n\nOption B: It's unclear. If Mr. Zhang is in good health, he shouldn't have filled in \"health\" in the health column. Maybe he intended to declare something else but made a mistake. Alternatively, if he didn't fill it out correctly, that could be a problem. But the wording is a bit confusing. It says he filled in the word \"health,\" which might be incorrect because he's in good health. So, maybe he misrepresented his health status by filling in \"health\" when he should have left it blank or indicated good health. That could be a violation.\n\nOption C: If Zhang didn't inform the insurance company about his congenital disease, that's a violation. But the option doesn't specify whether he did inform or not. It just says the father asked for reimbursement, which might mean the insurance company didn't know.