'm missing something.\n\nWait, perhaps the correct answer is C. Because if the apprenticeship length has declined, then carpenters might not have had as much training, making them less skilled. So if they were less skilled, then the writer's argument that older carpenters were more skilled would be weakened. So C weakens the argument.\n\nWait, but earlier I thought C supports the argument. Now I'm confused. Let me clarify.\n\nThe writer says that older hotels have better carpentry because older carpenters were more skilled. If the apprenticeship length has decreased, then maybe the skill level has decreased. So if C is true, then the writer's conclusion that older carpenters were more skilled is less likely to be true because their training was shorter. Therefore, C weakens the argument.\n\nBut wait, the question is which option weakens the argument. So if C is true, it weakens the writer's claim that older carpenters were more skilled because their training was shorter, implying they were less skilled.\n\nAlternatively, if D is true, it means that the materials were the same, so the quality of work is due to skill. Therefore, if D is true, it supports the writer's argument because the same materials would mean that better work is due to better skill. So D doesn't weaken the argument.\n\nWait, but the question is which option weakens the argument. So if D is true, it doesn't weaken the argument; it supports it. So the correct answer must be C because it weakens the argument by suggesting that the skill level of carpenters decreased over time.\n\nWait, but earlier I thought C supports the argument. Now I'm getting conflicting thoughts. Let me try again.\n\nThe writer's argument: Older hotels have better carpentry because older carpenters were more skilled.\n\nIf C is true, the average apprenticeship length has declined. So if apprenticeships are shorter, carpenters might not have as much training, so they might be less skilled. Therefore, the writer's conclusion that