      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out this logic problem. Let me read it again carefully.\n\nThe context says that North America has been shifting its main energy source from wood to coal, then to oil and natural gas. Each time, the new fuel has less carbon and more hydrogen than the previous one. The argument concludes that in the future, the main energy source will be pure hydrogen.\n\nThe question is asking which of the options A to D expresses a general principle that underlies this argument.\n\nFirst, I need to understand the argument's structure. It's talking about a series of changes in energy sources. Each step, the new fuel has less carbon and more hydrogen. So, starting from wood, which has more carbon, moving to coal (less carbon than wood, more hydrogen), then oil and natural gas (even less carbon, more hydrogen). The trend is clear: each new fuel has less carbon and more hydrogen than the previous.\n\nThe argument then concludes that eventually, the main energy source will be pure hydrogen. That makes sense because if each step increases hydrogen and decreases carbon, eventually, you might end up with something that's 100% hydrogen, assuming this trend continues.\n\nNow, looking at the options:\n\nOption A: If each step in a series of changes involves a decrease of one attribute and an increase of another, the series will terminate with the first attribute eliminated and only the second present.\n\nThis seems to fit. The argument is saying that each step removes carbon (the first attribute) and adds hydrogen (the second). So, over time, the energy source will have less and less carbon until it's gone, leaving only hydrogen.\n\nOption B: If the second stage of a process has been completed more quickly than the first stage, the third stage will be completed more quickly than the second.\n\nThis doesn't seem related. The context is about attributes of energy sources, not about the speed of stages in a process.\n\nOption C: If each of two desirable attributes belongs to a useful substance, the most useful form will have those attributes in equal amounts.\n\nThis talks about balancing two desirable attributes, but the argument is about a decrease and increase of attributes, not balancing them.\n\nOption D: If one substance is better for a purpose than another, the best substance includes all attributes of the first and none of the second.\n\nThis seems like it's about combining attributes, but the argument is more about a progression where one attribute is decreasing and another is increasing, not combining them.\n\nSo, the correct principle is in Option A because it directly relates to the trend of decreasing one attribute and increasing another, leading to the elimination of the first and dominance of the second.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: A\n\nThe argument describes a series of changes where each step decreases carbon (first attribute) and increases hydrogen (second attribute). According to Option A, this trend will result in the elimination of carbon and dominance of hydrogen, which aligns with the argument's conclusion.\n\nThe correct option is: A",
