 is the best strategy because wealthy individuals might not need as much coverage, or maybe they have other insurance already, which could complicate things.\n\nOption C suggests insuring only those individuals who were rejected by other companies for similar policies. That's interesting. If other companies rejected them, it might mean that they are higher risk or have more severe conditions. Insuring only those could lead to higher claims, which would increase the company's losses. So this seems like the opposite of what they want. They would be taking on people who are more likely to make claims, which would hurt their finances.\n\nOption D is about attracting middle-aged customers who are unlikely to submit claims for many years. Middle-aged people generally have lower medical needs compared to the elderly, so if they're insuring them, the claims might come in later, but the company is concerned about claims now. However, if they're targeting middle-aged customers, maybe the claims would come in sooner, but the premium is low enough to attract them. Wait, but the policy is for elderly people who have diseases. So maybe this option is not relevant because the policy is specifically for elderly people, not middle-aged. So insuring middle-aged people might not align with the target demographic.\n\nWait, but the policy is for elderly people with certain diseases, so maybe the company should focus on that group. So looking back at the options, Option A is about excluding those with pre-existing conditions, but the policy is for elderly people who do have such diseases. So maybe Option A isn't the best because it's excluding the people who need the policy. Option B is about wealthy individuals, which might have higher coverage ability, but I'm not sure if that's the best approach. Option C is taking on higher risk individuals, which would increase claims. Option D is about middle-aged people, which doesn't fit the target.\n\nWait, maybe I'm misunderstanding. The policy