ay out. But I'm not sure if that's the case.\n\nWait, perhaps the correct answer is Option A because by excluding those with pre-existing conditions, the company reduces the number of claims, thus minimizing losses. But I'm not entirely confident because the policy is for elderly people with these diseases, so maybe they should include those people. Alternatively, maybe the company should focus on those who are more likely to pay higher premiums, thus reducing the claims relative to the premiums.\n\nI'm a bit confused. Let me think again. The company wants to minimize losses, which means they want to have premiums high enough to cover claims. If they set premiums too low, they might not attract enough customers, but if they set them too high, they might lose customers. So, to minimize losses, they need to balance the premiums and the claims.\n\nOption A: Insuring only those without pre-existing conditions. This would reduce the risk of claims, so the company would have lower claims, which is good. But the policy is for elderly people with these diseases, so excluding them might limit the customer base, but perhaps the premium can be set lower, which might still be attractive enough.\n\nOption B: Insuring wealthy individuals. They can pay higher premiums, so the company can charge more, which would reduce the claims as a percentage of premiums. But if the company sets low premiums, maybe the wealthy can still afford it, but the claims would be smaller relative to the premiums.\n\nOption C: Insuring rejected by others. This would increase claims, which is bad for the company.\n\nOption D: Middle-aged customers. They don't fit the target, so this is irrelevant.\n\nSo, between A and B, which is better? If the company insures only those without pre-existing conditions, they might have lower claims, but they might also have a smaller customer base. Alternatively, insuring wealthy individuals would have higher coverage, so the company can charge more, reducing the claims as a percentage of premiums.\n\nBut the question is about minimizing losses, which is about the actual amount paid out. So, if the company can charge higher premiums, the total losses would be lower because the claims would be a smaller portion of the total premiums collected.\n\nWait, but the company is concerned that the income won't cover the claims. So, if they can charge higher premiums, the total income would be higher, reducing the losses. So, Option B might be better because they can charge more, thus reducing the claims relative to the premiums.\n\nAlternatively, if they only take on low-risk individuals, they might have lower claims, but perhaps the premium can be set lower, which might not be enough to attract enough customers.\n\nI'm still a bit confused, but I think the best answer is Option A because by excluding those with pre-existing conditions, the company reduces the risk of claims, thus minimizing losses. But I'm not entirely sure. Alternatively, Option B might be better because they can charge higher premiums, reducing the claims as a percentage of income.\n\nWait, maybe the correct answer is Option A because it reduces the number of claims, which directly addresses the company's concern. So, by only insuring those without pre-existing conditions, the company can have fewer claims, thus minimizing losses.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: A\n\nStep-by-step explanation:\nThe company wants to minimize losses by reducing the number of claims. Insuring only those without pre-existing conditions (Option A) reduces the risk of claims, directly addressing the concern. While Option B could reduce claims as a percentage of premiums, it might not be as effective in minimizing absolute losses. Options C and D are worse as they increase claims or target the wrong demographic. Therefore, Option A is the best strategy.\n\nThe correct option is: A",
