n\nWait, so both B and D seem relevant. Let me think again. The argument is about the importance of funding projects that have indirect benefits, even if their intended consequences don't directly help people. So B is about not preventing funding because the intended consequences don't benefit most, which is exactly what the argument is saying. D is more about the relationship between the goal's practicality and the consequences, which is also a point made but perhaps less directly.\n\nSo I think B is the best answer because it directly addresses the principle of supporting projects whose intended consequences aren't directly for most people, which is the core of the argument.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: B\n\nThe argument emphasizes that governments should continue funding space exploration because its unintended benefits, like fiber optics and computers, provide significant indirect advantages. Option B captures this principle by stating that governments shouldn't be prevented from allocating resources to projects whose intended consequences don't directly benefit most people, aligning with the argument's focus on indirect benefits.\n\nThe correct option is: B",
