a to lose some voters who support Israel. This seems to cover both possibilities. If the US uses its veto, it leads to anti-American sentiment. If it doesn't, it leads to Obama losing voters. So this statement combines both scenarios, meaning one of these two must happen regardless of the US's action. Therefore, C must be true.\n\nD) The UN General Assembly will vote to make Palestine an observer with statehood even if the US uses its veto. The context doesn't mention anything about the UN General Assembly's decision. It only talks about the Security Council's veto. So D is making an assumption about the UN GA that isn't supported by the given information.\n\nSo, putting it all together, the correct answer should be C because it covers both possible outcomes based on the US's actions, and it must be true given the context.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: C\n\nStep-by-step explanation:\n\n1. The context outlines two scenarios based on the US's actions:\n   - If the US uses its veto, anti-American sentiment in the Arab world increases.\n   - If the US doesn't use its veto, Israel is offended, causing Obama to lose voters.\n\n2. Option C combines both scenarios, stating that either anti-American sentiment will be ignited or Obama will lose voters. This covers all possibilities, making it necessarily true.\n\nAnswer: C",
