      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out this question about Mo Yan's literary works. Let me read the context again carefully. It says that Mo Yan's works have made the hidden Chinese vernacular known to the world and recognized by the international literary world. The judgment mentioned is that only the national is the world. Hmm, that's a bit abstract, but I think I get the gist.\n\nSo, the context is talking about how Mo Yan's literature has brought China's vernacular culture to the world, and this achievement is seen as proof that only the national (which I think refers to the nation or national identity) is considered the world. So, the idea is that the national is the ultimate or the only thing that can be recognized as the world.\n\nNow, the question is asking which of the options A to D is correct based on this information. Let me look at the options again:\n\nA) It's national, it's world\nB) If it is not national, it is not world\nC) As long as the nation is, it is the world\nD) Only the world is national\n\nI need to translate the context into logical statements and see which option aligns with it. The context seems to suggest that the recognition by the world (world view) is only possible through the nation (national identity). So, if something is recognized by the world, it must be national. Alternatively, if it's not national, it can't be recognized by the world.\n\nLet me break it down. The context says that Mo Yan's works have made the Chinese vernacular known to the world, and this is because only the national is the world. So, the connection is that the world (recognition) is tied to the nation (Chinese identity). So, if something is recognized by the world, it must be national. That translates to: If it's world (recognized), then it's national. Or in logical terms, World \u2192 National.\n\nLooking at the options, let's see which one matches this. Option B says, \"If it is not national, it is not world.\" That's the contrapositive of World \u2192 National. Because if World implies National, then not National implies not World. So, B is logically equivalent to the statement in the context.\n\nOption C says, \"As long as the nation is, it is the world.\" That sounds like saying National \u2192 World, which is the converse of what the context is saying. The context is World \u2192 National, not the other way around. So C might not be correct.\n\nOption A is a bit vague, \"It's national, it's world.\" It doesn't specify the direction of the implication. It could be interpreted either way, but the context is more about the implication from world to national.\n\nOption D says, \"Only the world is national.\" That sounds a bit off. It might mean that the only thing that can be national is the world, which doesn't quite fit. The context is about how the world is recognized because of the nation, not that the world is the only national thing.\n\nSo, putting it all together, the correct option should be B because it correctly captures the contrapositive of the statement in the context. If something isn't national, then it isn't recognized by the world. That aligns with the idea that only the national is the world.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: B\n\nStep-by-step explanation:\n1. The context states that Mo Yan's works have made the Chinese vernacular known to the world, and this is because only the national is the world.\n2. This implies that if something is recognized by the world (World), it must be national (National). Logically, this is represented as World \u2192 National.\n3. The contrapositive of this statement is If not National, then not World (\u00acNational \u2192 \u00acWorld), which is logically equivalent.\n4. Option B states, \"If it is not national, it is not world,\" which matches the contrapositive and thus aligns with the context.\n\nThe correct option is: B",
