g the eight-week training course.\nThis seems to align with the context. The context says that high school graduates who didn't earn the certificate had evening obligations. So, this is a direct restatement of the given information. Therefore, B must be true.\n\nOption C: Most of the workers who have earned the certificate are high school graduates.\nThe context says that nearly all high school graduates who completed the training earned the certificate. So, it's likely that most certificate holders are high school graduates. But wait, the context doesn't explicitly say that all high school graduates earned the certificate, only that those who did have evening obligations. So, maybe some high school graduates didn't earn it, but the majority who did would be high school graduates. However, the wording is a bit tricky. The context says \"nearly all\" high school diploma holders earned the certificate, so it's possible that most certificate holders are high school graduates. But I'm not entirely sure if this is necessarily true because the context doesn't specify the total number of high school graduates versus non-graduates. So, maybe C is a possible answer, but I'm not certain.\n\nOption D: No factory worker without a high school diploma has completed the course and earned the certificate.\nThis seems a bit off. The context says that high school graduates who completed the training earned the certificate. It doesn't say anything about workers without high school diplomas. So, it's possible that some factory workers without diplomas might have completed the training and earned the certificate. The context doesn't provide information about that. Therefore, D is making a statement that isn't necessarily supported by the given information.\n\nWait, let me think again about option C. The context says that nearly all high school diploma holders earned the certificate. So, if most of the certificate holders are high school graduates, then C would be true. But the context doesn't explicitly state that all non-graduates didn't earn the certificate. It only says that non-graduates couldn't attend the training. So, maybe some non-graduates could have earned the certificate through other means, but the context doesn't mention that. Therefore, it's possible that some non-graduates earned the certificate, which would mean that C isn't necessarily true.\n\nSo, going back, option B directly states what the context says, so it must be true. Option C is a bit uncertain because it's possible that some non-graduates earned the certificate, but the context doesn't confirm that. Therefore, the correct answer is B.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: B\n\nStep-by-step explanation:\n\n1. The context states that high school graduates who completed the training earned the certificate.\n2. Those who didn't earn the certificate had evening obligations preventing them from attending.\n3. Option B directly restates this information, confirming it must be true.\n4. Options A, C, and D are not supported by the given information.\n\nAnswer: B",
