      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out which of the given options is an example of Internet addiction based on the provided context. Let me start by understanding the context first. The context defines Internet addiction as a mental behavior disorder characterized by excessive use of the Internet, leading to a strong desire to use it again, withdrawal symptoms when stopping or reducing use, and possible physical or mental symptoms.\n\nNow, I'll go through each option one by one to see which fits this definition.\n\nOption A: Xiaoli processes text materials online every day and now avoids using paper and pens. This seems like regular online use, but does it indicate excessive use? The context doesn't specify a threshold, so just using the internet a bit might not be considered addiction yet.\n\nOption B: Xiao Wang likes online games and spends all day in internet cafes. If he doesn't use the internet for a day, he feels he's lost his soul. This shows heavy reliance and withdrawal symptoms, which are key signs of addiction. He's spending a lot of time online, which aligns with excessive use.\n\nOption C: After an internet company went bankrupt, Xiao Li was desperate and didn't know what to do. This seems more like a financial problem leading to stress or lack of options, not necessarily an addiction to the internet itself.\n\nOption D: Mr. Li is into stock speculation and watches the stock market online every day, feeling uneasy without it. While this is intense, it's more about trading and financial activities rather than the internet itself. It's possible, but maybe not as direct as online gaming.\n\nComparing all options, Option B clearly shows excessive use (spending all day in internet cafes) and withdrawal symptoms (feeling lost without internet), which fits the definition of Internet addiction best.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: B",
