      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out which of the options A, B, C, or D is an example of directional movement as defined in the context provided. Let me start by understanding the context thoroughly.\n\nThe context says that directional movement refers to the directional motion caused by external stimulation in a single direction. It mentions positive motion, gravity movement, tactile motion, and directional motion. Most of this movement is growth movement, which is an irreversible process.\n\nSo, directional movement is about how plants move in a specific direction due to some external factor. The key points are that it's caused by external stimulation, it's in a single direction, and it's related to growth movement, which is irreversible.\n\nNow, looking at the options:\n\nOption A: The puppy, lured repeatedly by the bone, sees the bone again and continues to droouff. Hmm, wait, \"droouff\" doesn't make much sense. Maybe it's a typo? It could be \"drouff,\" but that doesn't seem right. Alternatively, maybe it's \"drought,\" but that's not relevant. Alternatively, perhaps it's \"drowsy,\" but that doesn't fit either. I'm not sure, but this seems more about the puppy's behavior towards the bone, which is a stimulus, but it's not about plant movement. So probably not A.\n\nOption B: The roots of rice always grow in the direction of more fertilizer in the soil. Okay, this sounds familiar. I remember that roots grow towards higher concentrations of certain substances, like gravity (which is why roots fall) and also towards light (phototropism). But in this case, it's towards more fertilizer. So the roots are responding to the presence of fertilizer, which is an external stimulus. This seems like directional movement because the roots are moving in a specific direction (towards more fertilizer) due to external stimulation (the fertilizer). So B looks like a good candidate.\n\nOption C: The petals of the cow spread in the morning, and when the sun shines directly at noon, the petals close. Wait, \"cow\" is a mammal, and plants don't have petals. Maybe it's a typo, and they meant \"cow's flowers\" or \"cow's milk,\" but that doesn't make sense. Alternatively, perhaps it's referring to a plant, like a flower, which has petals. But the context says \"cow,\" which is confusing. Even if it's a typo, the petals closing when the sun is at noon is about light, which causes them to close, like in the case of some flowers to conserve water. But is that directional movement? It's more about a response to light, but it's a two-way movement\u2014opening in the morning and closing when light is strong. So it's not a single directional movement, but more of a response to light intensity. So maybe not C.\n\nOption D: Plants that prefer shade grow better in the shade than in direct sunlight. This is about plants' preference for certain environments. It's a growth habit, but it's not about movement in a specific direction due to an external stimulus. It's more about the plant's ability to thrive in a particular condition rather than moving in a certain direction. So D doesn't fit the definition.\n\nSo, putting it all together, the correct answer should be B because the roots of rice are growing in a specific direction (towards more fertilizer) due to external stimulation, which fits the definition of directional movement.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: B\n\nThe roots of rice grow in the direction of more fertilizer, which is an example of directional movement as they respond to the external stimulus of higher nutrient concentration.",
