s okay. So in this scenario, the first formation is D, B, G, and the second is A, C, E, H.\n\nBut wait, let's check if this setup violates any conditions.\n\n- Condition 1: The aircraft carrier is in the second formation. So if the second formation is A, C, E, H, then the aircraft carrier must be one of these. Let's assume the aircraft carrier is, say, A. Then condition 1 is satisfied.\n\n- Condition 2: E and C are not both in the first. In this case, neither is in the first, so that's fine.\n\n- Condition 3: A and C are not in the same formation. A is in the second, C is in the second, so that's fine.\n\n- Condition 4: If B is in the first, D is in the first. Yes, both are in the first.\n\nSo this setup works. Now, in this case, the first formation has D, B, G. So D is in the first, G is in the first, B is in the first, and the second has A, C, E, H.\n\nBut wait, the question is, if D and G are in the same formation, which option can be derived. So in this case, D is in the first, G is in the first. So the first formation is D, B, G.\n\nNow, looking at the options:\n\nA) A in the first formation: In this scenario, A is in the second, so A is not in the first. So A is false.\n\nB) B in the first formation: Yes, B is in the first, so B is true.\n\nC) C in the first formation: No, C is in the second, so C is false.\n\nD) E in the first formation: No, E is in the second, so D is false.\n\nSo in this case, option B is true.\n\nBut wait, let's consider the other case where D is in the second formation. So D is in the second, which has 4 ships. Then, condition 4 doesn't force B to be in the first because condition 4 is only if B is in the first, then D is in the first. So if D is in the second, B can be in either formation.\n\nBut since D is in the second, let's see what else is possible.\n\nSo second formation has D, plus three others. The first formation has 3 ships, which can't include A and C together, and E and C can't both be in the first.\n\nAlso, the aircraft carr