career elsewhere, while most people staying or moving in were adults with long-term jobs. This seems to talk about the reasons for moving in and out. But how does that strengthen the population decline? It might explain why the moving out is happening, but it doesn't directly address whether the number of people moving in is sufficient to offset the out-migration. So, maybe B doesn't directly strengthen the argument about population decline.\n\nOption C says that over the past century, any census showing a population loss was followed by a gain ten years later. This seems to suggest that population trends might reverse over time. But does that strengthen the argument? The argument is about the last ten years, and this option is about a longer-term trend. It might imply that population trends can change, but it doesn't directly support the idea that the population will decline next year. In fact, it might suggest that the population could increase again, which would weaken the argument. So, C might not be the right choice.\n\nOption D mentions that most adults moving out were parents with children, while most remaining or moving in were older adults living alone. This seems relevant because if the people moving out are parents with children, they might be contributing more to the population through their children staying. However, the argument is about the population decline, so if the people moving out are parents with children, and those children are staying, the population decline might not be as severe as it seems. Wait, but the argument is that the population has declined because more people left than came in. If the people moving out are parents with children, and the children are staying, that might mean that the number of people moving in is actually higher because the children are part of the population. But I'm a bit confused here. Let me think again.\n\nWait, the argument is that more people left than came in, leading to a decline. If the people moving out