cs help consumers. Maybe it's a bit tangential.\n\nC) Some me-too drugs turn out to be more effective than the drugs they were designed to imitate.\nThis is interesting. If generics are more effective, then they would be better than the original drugs. But wait, the argument is that me-too drugs reduce the price of the original drug, not necessarily that they are better. However, if generics are more effective, they might be preferred by consumers, which could lead to even more competition, potentially driving down prices further. This could indirectly support the argument, but I'm not sure if it's the strongest.\n\nD) If all pharmaceutical companies developed me-too drugs, fewer innovative drugs would be developed.\nThis suggests that if everyone makes generics, there would be less need for new drugs. But the editorial's point is that having generics can lower prices, not necessarily that new drugs are bad. However, this option is about the impact on the number of innovative drugs, which might not directly address the benefit to consumers. It's more about the effect on the industry rather than the consumer benefit.\n\nWait, maybe I'm missing something. The editorial's main point is that having me-too drugs can lead to price reductions. So the option that directly supports this would be the one that shows that having generics leads to lower prices, which in turn benefits consumers.\n\nLooking back, option C says that some generics are more effective. If they're more effective, consumers might prefer them, which could lead to the original drug's price dropping even more. But the editorial's argument is about price reduction, not necessarily about effectiveness. So maybe option C supports the argument by showing that generics can be better, leading to more competition and lower prices.\n\nOption B talks about companies losing money on innovative drugs because of generics. This might mean that companies are less inclined to invest in new drugs if they know generics are