the argument.\n\nAlternatively, option B says that companies lose money on innovative drugs because of generics. This might mean that companies are less likely to invest in new drugs, which could reduce the number of innovative drugs. But the editorial's argument is about the benefit to consumers, not about the number of innovative drugs. So maybe B doesn't directly support the argument.\n\nOption A says that companies make more money on generics. If they make more money, maybe they invest more in generics, but it doesn't necessarily lead to lower prices for the original drug.\n\nOption D says that if all companies make generics, fewer innovative drugs would be developed. This is more about the industry's future, not about the benefit to consumers.\n\nSo, considering all this, the best option that supports the argument is C, because if generics are more effective, consumers might prefer them, leading to lower prices for the original drug, thus benefiting consumers.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: C\n\nStep-by-step explanation:\nThe editorial argues that me-too drugs can benefit consumers by reducing the price of similar original drugs. Option C supports this by suggesting that some me-too drugs are more effective, which could lead to increased competition and potentially lower prices for original drugs, thereby benefiting consumers. \n\nAnswer: C",
