      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out which option would most reduce the actor's worries based on the given context. Let me start by understanding the context first.\n\nThe context talks about 3D stereoscopic technology being the cutting-edge in film technology. It mentions that even though the screen characters in 3D movies are computer-generated, their performances, including actions and expressions, are based on real actors' performances, similar to how makeup technology works in the digital age. However, it also notes that some actors are worried because computer-generated images and animations might replace live-action performances in the future.\n\nSo, the main point here is that while 3D technology uses computers for some aspects, the actors' performances are still crucial and based on real human actors. The actors are worried about their roles being replaced by computers, but the context doesn't seem to indicate that this replacement is happening anytime soon. Instead, it's more about the current state of 3D technology and how it's still relying on human actors for certain aspects.\n\nNow, the question is asking which of the options, if true, would most reduce the actor's worries. Let's look at each option one by one.\n\nOption A: All film directors can only communicate with real people, not with computers. Hmm, this seems to suggest that directors are limited in how they can work, perhaps affecting the creative process. But how does this relate to the actors' worries about computer-generated characters? It might affect the collaboration between directors and actors but doesn't directly address the actors' concerns about their roles being replaced.\n\nOption B: The shooting of any film depends on the choice of the producer, and the actors can keep up with the development of the times. This talks about the flexibility of the film industry and the actors' ability to adapt. If actors can keep up with technological advancements, it might mean