      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out this logic problem. Let me read it again to make sure I understand it correctly.\n\nThe context says that an advertising firm has campaigns for both TV and the Internet, and these campaigns are similar in size. Now, the TV audience is one thousand times bigger than the Internet audience. But here's the kicker: the firm makes two-thirds of its revenue from Internet ads. That seems a bit confusing because if TV has a much larger audience, you'd think it would bring in more revenue, right? So there's a paradox here that needs to be resolved.\n\nThe question is asking which statement, if true, would explain this paradox. The options are A, B, C, D. Let me go through each one.\n\nOption A says the internet and TV ads are identical. Hmm, that doesn't make much sense. If they were the same, why would the firm make more money from one? Maybe it's not about the ads being different but about how they're paid or the cost per view. So I'm not sure if this resolves the paradox.\n\nOption B claims the firm has more experience with TV ads. More experience might mean they're more efficient or charge more, but I'm not sure how that ties into the audience size and revenue. It might affect costs, but not necessarily the revenue ratio. So maybe not the right answer.\n\nOption C suggests the firm pays more for internet ads. Wait, if they pay more per ad, but they're getting more views from TV, how does that affect revenue? Let me think. If TV has a much larger audience, even if they charge less, the total revenue from TV could still be higher. But the firm is making more money from Internet ads, which is two-thirds of their revenue. So maybe if they pay more per ad on the Internet, they can get that two-thirds. But I'm not sure if this directly addresses the paradox because the audience size is so different.\n\nOption D states that Internet ads allow the firm to more accurately target audiences. That makes sense. If they can target better, they might get more relevant ads, which could mean higher engagement or more conversions. So even though the audience size is smaller, the quality of the audience is better, leading to higher revenue. This could explain why Internet ads, despite the smaller audience, bring in two-thirds of the revenue.\n\nWait, let me double-check. If the Internet audience is smaller but the firm can target it more effectively, maybe each ad on the Internet is more valuable because it's reaching a more specific and profitable group. So the revenue per ad on the Internet might be higher, making up for the smaller audience size. That would mean the total revenue from Internet ads could be a larger portion, which aligns with the given information.\n\nSo, putting it all together, the paradox is that despite a much larger audience, TV brings in less revenue. The only way that makes sense is if the Internet ads are more profitable per viewer or per ad. Option D explains that by targeting more accurately, so each ad is worth more, hence the higher revenue from Internet ads.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: D\n\nThe firm makes more revenue from Internet ads despite a smaller audience because Internet ads allow for more accurate targeting, leading to higher engagement or conversions per viewer. This explains why Internet ads can generate a larger portion of revenue despite having a smaller audience.\n\nThe correct option is: D",
