I'm not sure how that ties into the argument about rejecting 1%. Unless it's about whether the cheese is produced domestically, but the argument is more about the cost-benefit of rejection.\n\nD) Does rejecting cheese deter exporters from shipping substandard cheese? This seems directly relevant because if rejection leads to deterrence, then the 1% rejection rate might actually be effective, providing some benefit. If it deters many exporters, then the 1% rejection could be seen as having value, which would impact the argument that costs outweigh benefits.\n\nSo, evaluating the argument, the key is whether rejecting that 1% actually has real consequences. Option D addresses whether rejection deters exporters, which would mean that the 1% rejection is effective and thus has some benefit. If it doesn't deter, then maybe the 1% rejection isn't that significant, making the argument stronger.\n\nOption B is also relevant because it's about the agency's costs. If they've reduced costs, the argument that costs outweigh benefits might be weaker. But the argument is more about the benefits of rejection, so D seems more directly relevant.\n\nTherefore, the most useful option is D.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: D\n\nThe reasoning is that the argument focuses on whether the 1% rejection rate has real consequences. Option D directly addresses whether rejection deters exporters, which would indicate the rejection's effectiveness and thus its potential benefits.",
