      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out which option does not belong to the broken window effect based on the given definition. Let me start by understanding the concept properly.\n\nThe broken window effect is a psychological phenomenon where if one part of a structure is damaged, it encourages others to follow suit, leading to a complete collapse of the structure. So, it's about how a small initial problem can lead to a larger problem over time because people see it as a trend or example they can follow.\n\nNow, looking at the options provided, I need to determine which one doesn't fit this pattern. Let's go through each option one by one.\n\nOption A: If the graffiti on a wall is not cleaned, the wall will soon be full of messy and ugly things. This seems to fit the broken window effect. Graffiti is a visible, small problem that, once present, can encourage others to add more, leading to a messier environment. So, this is an example of the broken window effect.\n\nOption B: If a tributary is polluted and not treated, it may pollute the water quality of the whole basin. This also fits. A polluted tributary is like a broken window because once it's polluted, it can lead to the entire water system being polluted over time. So, this is another example of the broken window effect.\n\nOption C: Once there is rubbish in a very clean place, people will throw their rubbish away without hesitation and will not feel ashamed. Hmm, this seems a bit different. The broken window effect is about a small initial problem leading to more problems. But here, the presence of rubbish in a clean place is causing people to throw more rubbish, which might be more of a personal or individual issue rather than a trend that spreads. It's more about personal habits or lack of responsibility rather than a societal trend that spreads. So, this might not fit the broken window effect.\n\nOption D: If a few people in a group speak uncivilized languages and are not stopped, many people will soon speak uncivilized languages. This definitely fits. A few people speaking uncivilized languages can become a trend, leading others to follow suit, which is exactly the broken window effect.\n\nSo, putting it all together, options A, B, and D all illustrate the broken window effect where a small problem leads to a larger one. Option C, however, is about personal behavior and doesn't involve a trend or influence from others in the same way. Therefore, option C does not belong to the broken window effect.\n</think>\n\nThe broken window effect involves a small initial problem leading to a larger issue through imitation. Options A, B, and D fit this pattern, while C is about personal habits and doesn't spread like a trend.\n\nThe correct option is: C",
