ut the absence of creativity in some knowledgeable people.\n\nD) People with profound knowledge may not have the ability to create.\nThis seems to align with the context. The context is saying that knowledgeable scholars (who presumably have profound knowledge) may not have the ability to create. So, this is directly supported by the context.\n\nWait, but let me think again. The context says that some knowledgeable scholars lack creative ability. So, does that mean that people with profound knowledge may not be able to create? Yes, that's what D is saying. So, D is supported.\n\nBut let me check the options again. Option C is about creative people not having profound knowledge, but the context is about knowledgeable people (who presumably have profound knowledge) lacking creativity. So, C is about creative people, which isn't directly addressed in the context. The context is more about knowledgeable people lacking creativity.\n\nSo, the correct answer should be D.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: D\n\nThe context states that knowledgeable scholars, despite their effort, may lack creative ability. This supports the argument that people with profound knowledge may not have the ability to create.\n\nThe correct option is: D",
