      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out which of the options A to D must be true based on the given context. The context says that any paper involving national military, political, or economic secrets cannot be made public. So, if a paper has any of those secrets, it's not public. \n\nLet me break this down. The statement is a universal one. It's saying that if a paper involves any of those three areas, it's not public. So, it's a rule that applies to all such papers. Now, I need to see which of the options must also be true based on this.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nOption A: Papers that do not involve national military, political, or economic secrets cannot be made public. Hmm, wait. The original statement says that papers involving those secrets cannot be public. But it doesn't say anything about papers that don't involve those secrets. So, maybe they can be public or not. The original statement doesn't restrict papers without those secrets from being public. So A might not necessarily be true.\n\nOption B: All unpublic papers are about the national military, political, or economic secrets. The original statement says that if a paper involves those secrets, it's not public. But it doesn't say that all unpublic papers must involve those secrets. There could be other reasons why a paper isn't public, like being fictional or not involving any secrets. So B might not be true.\n\nOption C: Some papers involving national military, political, or economic secrets can be made public. Wait, the original statement says that any paper involving those secrets cannot be made public. So if a paper involves those secrets, it can't be public. So C is saying that some of them can be made public, which contradicts the original statement. So C is false.\n\nOption D: None of the public papers are involved in national military, political, or economic secrets. This seems to align with the original statement. If a paper is public, it doesn't involve any of those secrets. So D must be true.\n\nWait, let me double-check. The original statement is a universal rule: if a paper involves those secrets, it's not public. So the contrapositive would be: if a paper is public, it doesn't involve those secrets. That's exactly what D is saying. So D must be true.\n\nSo the correct answer should be D.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: D\n\nStep-by-Step Explanation:\n\n1. The original statement asserts that any paper involving national military, political, or economic secrets cannot be made public.\n2. This means that if a paper has any of these secrets, it is excluded from public release.\n3. The contrapositive of this statement is that if a paper is public, it does not involve any of these secrets.\n4. Option D directly states this contrapositive, making it the correct choice.\n\nThe correct option is: D",
