n still improve because people can increase their consumption beyond necessities. So Option C doesn't directly address whether prices hitting rock bottom would prevent increased spending, but it does support the idea that the economy can improve because some spending is necessary.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the correct answer is Option D because it suggests that even when prices are low, people can save more, which would mean they can't increase consumption. But that would support Li Ming, not weaken him.\n\nWait, I'm getting more confused. Let me try to think of it differently.\n\nLi Ming's rebuttal is that if prices hit rock bottom, it won't lead to economic improvement because people can't increase consumption.\n\nTo weaken this, we need to show that even when prices are at rock bottom, people can still increase their consumption.\n\nLooking at the options, Option C doesn't directly address this, but Option D seems to suggest that when prices are low, people have more money to save, which would mean they can't increase consumption. So that supports Li Ming, not weaken him.\n\nWait, perhaps I'm overcomplicating this. Let me think about the structure of the arguments.\n\nZhang Qiang: Depression leads to job losses, reduced spending, more job losses, leading to economic depression. Prices hit rock bottom.\n\nLi Ming: If people lose jobs, they can't increase consumption. So if prices hit rock bottom, it won't help the economy.\n\nTo weaken Li Ming, we need to show that even when prices are at rock bottom, people can still increase their consumption.\n\nLooking at the options, Option C says that even in extreme downturns, the unemployed must buy necessities. This supports Zhang Qiang because it shows that some spending is necessary, but it doesn't directly show that people can increase their consumption beyond that. So it doesn't directly address Li Ming's point.\n\nOption D: During economic downturns, people cut back on spending, but when prices hit rock bottom, they ha