      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to solve this logic problem. Let me read through the context again to make sure I understand it.\n\nThe context says three things:\n\n1. If absolute fairness is implemented, it will inevitably lead to distribution according to work.\n2. If distribution according to work happens, there will be inequality between the rich and the poor.\n3. Only by equalizing the rich and the poor can absolute fairness be implemented.\n\nHmm, that's a bit confusing. Let me break it down step by step.\n\nFirst, the first statement is an \"if-then\" proposition. It says that if we implement absolute fairness (let's call this AF), then the distribution will be according to work (let's call this D). So AF \u2192 D.\n\nThe second statement is another \"if-then.\" If distribution according to work happens (D), then there will be inequality between the rich and the poor (let's call this I). So D \u2192 I.\n\nThe third statement is a bit trickier. It says that only by equalizing the rich and the poor can absolute fairness be implemented. That sounds like a necessary condition. So for AF to be possible, I must be true. In logical terms, that would be I \u2192 AF. Because only if I is true can AF happen.\n\nWait, let me think again. The wording is \"Only by equalizing the rich and the poor can absolute fairness be implemented.\" So that means equalizing the rich and poor is a necessary condition for AF. So AF can only happen if I is true. So AF \u2192 I. Or is it I \u2192 AF? Hmm, maybe I got that backwards.\n\nWait, no. \"Only by X can Y happen\" translates to Y \u2192 X. Because Y can only happen if X is true. So if AF can only be implemented if I is true, then AF \u2192 I. Because AF can't happen unless I is true.\n\nWait, no, that's not right. Let me clarify. If the statement is \"Only by X can Y happen,\" it means that Y can only happen if X is true. So Y \u2192 X. So in this case, AF can only be implemented if I is true. So AF \u2192 I.\n\nBut wa