      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out this question based on the given context. Let me read through it again to make sure I understand.\n\nThe context is about whether we should charge for the deployment of public resources to rescue tourism explorers who commit intentional adventures. Some people think we should charge, others don't. The question is asking which option cannot support the positive side's viewpoint. The options are A, B, C, D.\n\nFirst, I need to understand what the positive side's argument is. They probably think that since these adventurers are causing problems and wasting resources, they should pay for the rescue. So, the positive side would argue that charging is fair because it's a cost that should be covered by those who benefit from the service.\n\nNow, looking at each option:\n\nOption A: \"A rational citizen should be responsible for his own actions; otherwise, it will be unfair to other taxpayers.\" This seems to suggest that if someone causes harm, they should pay for it. So, if a tourist is in trouble, they should pay for the rescue. This supports the positive side's point because it's about holding the responsible party accountable.\n\nOption B: \"The establishment of a professional rescue system requires a certain amount of financial support.\" This is about why a rescue system needs funding. If the system requires financial support, then presumably, those who use it (the adventurers) should contribute. This supports the positive side because it's about the necessity of charging to sustain the system.\n\nOption C: \"It is the duty of government departments to rescue people in distress. Simple charges can not help adventurers build up a sense of social responsibility.\" Hmm, this is interesting. The positive side would argue that charging helps build responsibility, but this option is saying the opposite. It suggests that charging doesn't help the adventurers understand social responsibility. So, if this is true, it actually contradicts the positive side's argument. It means that charging might not be effective in promoting responsibility, so the positive side's point of view isn't supported by this.\n\nOption D: \"Tourist explorers who illegally enter unsafe areas and waste social resources and taxpayers' money should bear the corresponding expenses.\" This supports the positive side because it directly links the adventurers' actions to needing to pay for the rescue. They caused harm, so they should pay.\n\nSo, the question is asking which option cannot support the positive side. That would be the one that argues against charging. Option C is the only one that doesn't support the positive side's viewpoint.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: C",
