      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out this logic problem. Let me read it again carefully.\n\nThe context says that a smoker trying to quit is more likely to succeed if the doctor greatly exaggerates the dangers of smoking. This strategy is similar for breaking other habits. However, the argument points out that since these strategies involve deception, people can't easily adopt them unless a third party like a doctor provides the warning.\n\nThe question is asking which assumption the argument depends on. The options are A, B, C, D.\n\nFirst, I need to break down the argument. The main point is that exaggerating dangers (deception) can help people quit smoking, and this approach can be applied to other habits. But the catch is that people can't easily do this themselves unless someone else, like a doctor, provides the warning. So the argument is about the role of external warnings and deception in habit breaking.\n\nNow, looking at the options:\n\nA) The more the relevant danger is exaggerated, the more likely one is to break one's habit.\n\nThis seems to suggest that exaggeration leads to success. But the argument doesn't explicitly state this as an assumption. It's more about the necessity of external warnings rather than the effect of exaggeration itself.\n\nB) People generally do not find it easy to deceive themselves.\n\nThis seems relevant because the argument mentions that without external warnings, people can't easily deceive themselves. So this might be an assumption because the argument relies on the idea that self-deception is difficult, making external warnings necessary.\n\nC) A doctor is justified in deceiving a patient whenever doing so is likely to make the patient healthier.\n\nThis is about the justification of a doctor's actions. The argument doesn't directly address whether doctors are justified or not, just that they provide warnings. So this might not be the assumption.\n\nD) Most of the techniques that help people quit smoking can also help people break other habits.\n\nThe argument says that the strategy of exaggerating dangers can be used for other habits, so this seems like a point the argument makes, not an assumption. It's more of a conclusion than an assumption.\n\nSo, putting it together, the argument relies on the idea that people find it hard to deceive themselves, which is option B. Without this assumption, the argument wouldn't hold because it wouldn't explain why external warnings are necessary.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: B\n\nStep-by-step explanation:\n\nThe argument hinges on the difficulty people face in deceiving themselves, which is option B. Without this assumption, the necessity of external warnings (like a doctor's) wouldn't be justified, as the argument states that such strategies involve deception and are only effective when provided by a third party. \n\nAnswer: The correct option is: B",
