\nThe question asks which option correctly describes David's choice. The options are about extra money in two years or extra per year.\n\nWait, maybe I should calculate the difference per half-year and see the average extra per year.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the question is about the annual salary and the increase, not the total over two years.\n\nWait, let me think differently. Maybe the question is asking about the difference in the salary structure, not the total over two years.\n\nLet me see the options again:\n\nA) Cantor, extra $150,000 in two years.\n\nB) Huizhong, extra $50,000 a year.\n\nC) Cantor, extra $50,000 a year.\n\nD) Huizhong, extra $70,000 a year.\n\nWait, perhaps I should calculate the difference in the annual salary and the increase.\n\nWait, for Huizhong, the annual salary is effectively $1,000,000 (since two half-years of $500,000 each). The increase is $50,000 every six months, so $100,000 per year.\n\nFor Cantor, the annual salary is $1,000,000, and the increase is $200,000 per year.\n\nSo the difference in annual salary is $0 (both are $1,000,000), but the difference in increases is $100,000 per year (Cantor gives more). So over two years, Cantor would give an extra $200,000, while Huizhong gives an extra $100,000. Wait, that doesn't match my earlier calculation.\n\nWait, maybe I'm getting confused. Let me recast it.\n\nWait, perhaps the question is about the total over two years, not per year. So let's recalculate.\n\nHuizhong over two years: $500k + $550k + $600k + $650k = $2,300k.\n\nCantor over two years: $1,000k + $1,200k = $2,200k.\n\nSo the difference is $100k over two years, meaning an extra $50k per year on average. But wait, that's not exactly accurate because the increases are happening every six months.\n\nWait, perhaps the correct way is to calculate the difference each half-year.\n\nLet me list each half-year:\n\nHalf 1: Both pay $500k.\n\nHalf 2: Cantor pays $500k, Huizhong pays $550k. So Huizhong is better by $50k.\n\nHalf 3: Cantor pays $600k, Huizhong pays $600k. Equal.\n\nHalf 4: Cantor pays $600k, Huizhong pays $650k. Huizhong better by $50k.\n\nSo over two years, Huizhong is better by $100k in total, which is $50k per year on average.\n\nBut looking at the options, none of them mention $50k per year. Option C says Cantor gives an extra $50k a year, which is incorrect because Cantor actually gives more, but the difference is $100k over two years, which averages to $50k per year. Wait, but the question is about which company David will choose, not the difference.\n\nWait, perhaps I'm overcomplicating. Let me think about the options again.\n\nOption D says Huizhong gives an extra $70k a year. That doesn't match my calculation of $100k over two years, which is $50k per year. So maybe I'm missing something.\n\nWait, perhaps the question is about the difference in the annual salary and the increase. Let me see:\n\nHuizhong's annual salary is $1,000,000 (two half-years of $500k each). The increase is $50k every six months, so $100k per year.\n\nCantor's annual salary is $1,000,000, and the increase is $200k per year.\n\nSo the difference in annual salary is $0, but the difference in increases is $100k per year (Cantor gives more). So over two years, Cantor would give an extra $200k, while Huizhong gives an extra $100k. But that's not one of the options.\n\nWait, perhaps the question is about the total over two years, and the options are about which company gives more and by how much.\n\nSo Huizhong gives $2,300k, Cantor gives $2,200k. So Huizhong is better by $100k over two years. But none of the options mention $100k. So perhaps I'm misunderstanding the options.\n\nWait, let me look at the options again:\n\nA) Cantor, extra $150k in two years. No, I calculated $100k.\n\nB) Huizhong, extra $50k a year. That would be $100k over two years, which matches the total difference. So B says extra $50k a year, which over two years is $100k. So that's correct.\n\nWait, but earlier I thought the d