      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out which option best completes the argument given in the context. Let me break it down step by step.\n\nFirst, the context talks about Utrania's oil exports. They used to be a major exporter, but now they're struggling. The reasons are economic stagnation and restrictive regulations, which have stopped new investments in oil fields. Because of this, their exports have been dropping as the old fields run out. But now, Utrania's economy is improving, and the regulations are becoming less restrictive, so they expect new fields to develop rapidly. However, the argument points out that it's premature to conclude that these new fields will lead to higher oil exports. So, I need to find why that might not be the case.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nA) New technology can recover oil from old depleted fields. This seems relevant because if old fields can be used again, that could increase exports without needing new fields. So, this could explain why even with new fields, exports might not rise as expected.\n\nB) Economic improvement leads to more people owning cars. While more cars might increase demand, it's not directly related to oil exports. The argument is about oil production, not consumption. So, this might not be the best fit.\n\nC) Oil price stability. If oil prices are stable, higher production could lead to higher exports. But the argument is about why we can't conclude that new fields will increase exports, so this might not be the main issue. Plus, the context doesn't mention anything about price stability.\n\nD) Foreign investment in new fields. If most investment comes from outside, it might affect the economy, but again, the argument is about the relationship between new fields and exports. This might not directly address the issue.\n\nSo, the key point is that even with new fields, if old ones can still be used, exports might not go up. That makes option A the most logical choice because it introduces another factor that could influence oil exports without necessarily relying on new fields.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: A\n\nThe argument highlights that while Utrania's economic improvement and less restrictive regulations are expected to lead to rapid development of new oil fields, it's premature to assume this will result in higher oil exports. The most logical completion is that new technology might allow recovery from old, depleted fields, which could mean higher exports without new investments. \n\nAnswer: A",
