      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out which principle best explains the situation described in the context. Let me break it down step by step.\n\nFirst, the context says that when a major political scandal happens before an election, voters blame the scandal on all parties equally. In this case, all incumbents, regardless of their party, get re-elected. That makes sense because if the scandal is spread across all parties, voters might not have a clear favorite to blame, so they stick with the current incumbents.\n\nBut then, if the voters blame the scandal on only one party, that party's incumbents are likely to lose. So, in this scenario, the party that's being blamed loses a lot, and other parties' challengers win. This seems to be about how voters perceive responsibility and how that affects the election outcome.\n\nNow, looking at the question, it's asking which principle explains this contrast. The options are A, B, C, D. Let me go through each one.\n\nOption A says that major political scandals can practically always be blamed on incumbents, but whether they should be removed depends on their challengers. Hmm, that doesn't quite fit. The context doesn't talk about whether the scandals should be blamed on incumbents or not, just how voters distribute blame. So maybe A isn't the best fit.\n\nOption B states that blaming incumbents from all parties is more accurate than blaming one party. Wait, the context doesn't mention accuracy of blame. It's more about the reaction of voters and the election outcome. So B might not be the right choice either.\n\nOption C suggests that when scandals are more the responsibility of parties, the responsible party must be penalized. This aligns with the context because when the scandal is blamed on one party, that party's incumbents face defeat. So this seems relevant.\n\nOption D says that incumbents who are rightly blamed shouldn't seek re-election, but if they do, they shouldn't be returned. This is about the consequences of being blamed, but the context doesn't specify whether the blame is correct or not. It just talks about how voters distribute blame and the election results. So D might not be the best fit.\n\nPutting it all together, the principle that explains the contrast is about penalizing the party that's blamed for the scandal. So the correct answer should be C.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: C\n\nThe principle that best explains the contrast is that when major political scandals are more the responsibility of a particular party, that party must be penalized when possible. This aligns with the context where voters' reactions lead to incumbents from the blamed party being defeated.\n\nThe correct option is: C",
