and 3 to SALES.\n\nWait, PRO already has F and X, so we need one more. Let's say the third in PRO is W. Then W is in PRO.\n\nBut condition 3: If X is in SALES, W is in PRO. But X is in PRO, so condition 3 doesn't apply. So W can be in PRO or elsewhere, but if W is in PRO, that's fine.\n\nAlternatively, W could be in PR or SALES, but let's see.\n\nWait, let's try to assign W to PRO. So PRO would have F, X, W. That's three in PRO.\n\nThen, the remaining departments:\n\nPR: 1 person from G, H, I, Y.\n\nSALES: 3 people from G, H, I, Y.\n\nBut H and Y must be together, so they can be in SALES or PR.\n\nWait, but PR only needs one person. So if H and Y are both in SALES, that's two in SALES, and then we need one more in SALES from G or I.\n\nWait, let's try:\n\nPRO: F, X, W.\n\nSALES: H, Y, and one more (G or I).\n\nPR: G or I.\n\nBut condition 2: F and G cannot be in the same department. Since F is in PRO, G can be in PR or SALES.\n\nIf G is in PR, then PR has G, and SALES has H, Y, and I.\n\nThat works.\n\nAlternatively, if G is in SALES, then SALES would have G, H, Y, which is three, but then PR would have I, which is fine.\n\nWait, but let's check condition 2: F and G cannot be in the same department. Since F is in PRO, G can be in PR or SALES. So both options are possible.\n\nNow, let's see the options given:\n\nA) G is assigned to the sales department.\n\nIs this possible? Yes, as in the scenario where G is in SALES, along with H and Y.\n\nB) H is assigned to the production department.\n\nWait, PRO already has F, X, W. So H can't be in PRO. So H must be in SALES or PR.\n\nIf H is in PRO, that's not possible because PRO is already full. So B is not possible.\n\nWait, but let me check again. If H is in PRO, then PRO would have F, X, W, H, which is four people, but PRO only needs three. So H can't be in PRO. So B is false.\n\nC) I was assigned to the sales department.\n\nYes, possible. For example, in the scenario where SALES has H, Y, I.\n\nD) W was assigned to the public relations department.\n\nWait, in our initial assignment, W is in PRO. But could W be in PR?\n\nLet me see. If W is in PR, then PRO would have F, X, and W. Wait, no, PR only needs one person. So if W is in PR, then PRO would have F and X, and W would be in PR, which is not possible because PRO needs three people. Wait, no, PRO needs three people, so if W is in PR, then PRO would have F, X, and someone else. But we only have three in PRO, so if W is in PR, then PRO would have F, X, and someone else, but that someone else would have to be in PRO, which would require that someone else to be in PRO, but we only have three spots.\n\nWait, maybe I'm getting confused. Let me re-examine.\n\nIf W is in PR, then PRO would have F and X, and we need one more in PRO. So the third person in PRO would have to be someone else, but who?\n\nThe remaining candidates are G, H, I, Y.\n\nIf W is in PR, then PRO has F, X, and someone else. Let's say the third in PRO is G. But condition 2 says F and G cannot be in the same department. So G can't be in PRO. So G can't be in PRO, so the third person in PRO must be someone else, but the only other candidates are H, I, Y.\n\nSo if W is in PR, then PRO would have F, X, and someone else, but that someone else can't be G. So let's say H is in PRO. But then H and Y must be together, so Y would also have to be in PRO. But that would make PRO have F, X, H, Y, which is four people, but PRO only needs three. So that's not possible.\n\nAlternatively, if W is in PR, then the third person in PRO would have to be someone else, but that's not possible because H and Y must be together, and if H is in PRO, Y must be too, making it four. So W can't be in PR because that would cause a conflict.\n\nTherefore, W must be in PRO, making option D impossible.\n\nWait, but earlier I thought that if W is in PRO, then D is possible, but now I'm getting that W can't be in PR because it would cause a problem. So D is not possible.\n\nWait, but let me check again. If