ES, so B is false. So B cannot be true.\n\nBut let me make sure. Let's try to see if H can be in PRO.\n\nIf H is in PRO, then Y must also be in PRO because of condition 1. So PRO would have F, X, H, Y. That's four people, but PRO only needs three. So that's impossible. Therefore, H cannot be in PRO. So B is impossible.\n\nSo the answer is B.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: B\n\nStep-by-step explanation:\n\n1. Assign F to PRO (condition 4).\n2. Assign X to PRO (since X is in the same department as F).\n3. Since X is in PRO, condition 3 doesn't apply, so W can be anywhere.\n4. Assign W to PR to test option D.\n5. Assign H and Y to SALES (condition 1).\n6. Assign G to SALES (since G can't be with F).\n7. Assign I to PRO (since PRO needs three).\n8. Check if H can be in PRO: It's impossible because PRO would have F, X, H, Y (four people).\n9. Therefore, H cannot be in PRO, making option B impossible.\n\nAnswer: B",
