c day, but I'm not sure.\n\nOption A is about religious observances. If they have a day off every seventh day, and the extra days are not part of any week, maybe their rest days would align differently. But I'm not sure if this would cause scheduling conflicts because their rest days would still be weekly, just not overlapping with the extra days.\n\nOption D is about three-day breaks on Mondays or Fridays. If the holidays shift, their breaks would be on different days, which could cause issues if they rely on those specific days off. But I'm not sure if this would cause continued conflicts because maybe they can adjust their schedules.\n\nWait, the question is about which group would still face scheduling conflicts. If the extra days are not part of any week, the weekdays shift each year. So, for people who plan events several years in advance, their plans would be based on shifted days, leading to conflicts because the same date next year would not fall on the same day of the week. So, option C seems to be the correct answer because they have to plan events far in advance, and the shifting weekdays would make it hard to align their schedules.\n\nAlternatively, option B might also be affected because December 31 would be a Sunday every year, but if the year is 365 days, December 31 would normally shift by one day each year. So, without the extra day, it would shift, but with the proposal, it's fixed. So, maybe people with anniversaries on December 31 would have their dates on Sundays every year, which could be a problem if they have events on Sundays, but I'm not sure if that's a scheduling conflict.\n\nWait, the original context says that without the extra day, anniversaries don't fall on the same day each year. So, with the proposal, maybe anniversaries would fall on the same day each year because the extra days are not part of any week. So, people with anniversaries on December 30 or 31 would have their dates on the same day each year, which might not be a conflict if they don't have events on those specific days. But I'm not sure.\n\nI think the key is that the proposal fixes the weekday alignment, so people who have events that depend on specific weekdays would not have conflicts. But the question is about which group would still face scheduling conflicts. So, maybe it's the opposite. If the weekdays shift, then people who rely on specific days would have issues. But the proposal is supposed to fix that by making the extra days non-week days, so maybe the conflict is resolved. Wait, no, the question is asking which group would still face conflicts if the proposal is put in place.\n\nWait, the original context says that without the extra day, anniversaries don't fall on the same day each year, causing scheduling conflicts. The proposal is to add extra days that are not part of any week, so that January 1 is always a Sunday. So, if the proposal is implemented, the weekdays would shift each year because the extra days are not part of any week, meaning that the same date would fall on a different day of the week each year. Therefore, people who plan events several years in advance (option C) would have their events on different weekdays each year, causing scheduling conflicts because their plans wouldn't align with others.\n\nAlternatively, if the extra days are not part of any week, the weekdays shift, so people who have anniversaries on December 30 or 31 would have their dates on the same day each year, which might not cause conflicts if they don't have events on those specific days. But I'm not sure.\n\nWait, the original context says that without the extra day, anniversaries don't fall on the same day each year, leading to scheduling conflicts. So, with the proposal, maybe the weekdays would shift each year, leading to the same problem. So, the group that would still face conflicts would be those who have events that depend on specific weekdays, like option C, because they have to plan events several years in advance, an