 the student is trying to apply that to a different scenario. The professor didn't say that B parents can't have O children; she only said O-O leads to O. So, the student might be extrapolating incorrectly here.\n\nD) If both parents have type B blood, their children will also have type B blood.\nThis is about both parents being B, which wasn't the case here. The student's father is B, but the other parent is O. So, this option is talking about a different scenario and doesn't directly relate to the professor's statement or the student's situation.\n\nPutting it all together, the student is challenging the professor's statement by providing a counterexample where one parent is B and the child is O. So, the student is interpreting the professor's statement as only O-O parents can have O children, which is technically correct based on the professor's statement, but the student is pointing out that other combinations can also result in O children. Therefore, the student's understanding is that only O parents can have O children, which is option A.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: A\n\nStep-by-step explanation:\n1. The professor stated that if both parents have type O blood, their children will only be type O.\n2. The student argues that this isn't true because her father is type B, yet she is type O.\n3. The student's understanding is that only those with type O blood can have children with type O blood, aligning with the professor's statement.\n4. The other options don't fit the context or the student's argument.\n\nAnswer: A",
