      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out this question based on the given context. Let me read through the context again to make sure I understand it properly.\n\nIn 2014, Beijing, Tianjin, and Hebei implemented the APEC governance model with the strictest emission reduction measures ever. The result was the \"APEC basket,\" which I think refers to a decline in pollution levels. But after the APEC meeting ended, the APEC blue (which I believe is a symbol of environmental improvement) faded. Some people are confused because the government used this model for a short time to get good results, so why not keep it up long-term?\n\nThe question is asking which option, when excluded, can explain the people's confusion. So, I need to figure out which of the options A, B, C, D doesn't contribute to explaining why people are confused.\n\nLet me break down each option:\n\nA) The most stringent emission reduction measures have created many practical difficulties that are difficult to solve in the process of implementation.\nThis makes sense because if the measures are too strict, they might cause problems that are hard to fix. Maybe implementing them long-term isn't feasible, so people are confused about why the model was only used temporarily.\n\nB) If the APEC governance model is normalized in the near future, it will seriously affect the local economic and social development.\nThis is another valid point. If the model is normalized, meaning it's put aside, it might disrupt local economies. So people would be concerned about the long-term effects, leading to confusion about why it was only used for a short time.\n\nC) Any environmental governance needs to pay a price, and the key lies in whether the price paid exceeds the benefits.\nThis seems a bit abstract. It's talking about the trade-offs in environmental policies. While this is a valid concern, it might not directly explain why the APEC model was only used temporarily. It's more about the value of environmental policies rather than the practical difficulties of implementing them.\n\nD) If haze occurs frequently during the APEC meeting, it will affect the image of our country.\nThis is a specific concern about public perception. If haze is frequent, it could damage the country's image. So, people might be worried about the long-term effects on their reputation, which could explain their confusion about why the model wasn't sustained.\n\nSo, the question is which option, when excluded, can still explain the confusion. The key is that options A, B, and D all directly address why the APEC model was only used temporarily. Option C is more about the value of environmental policies but doesn't directly explain the practical difficulties or the reasons for stopping the model. Therefore, excluding C would still leave A, B, and D to explain the confusion, making C the correct answer to exclude.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: C",
