      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out which option calls into question the argument made by the aviation analyst. Let me break it down step by step.\n\nFirst, the context says that Miro Jets installed anti-collision devices on their DC-10s. These devices use a red light to alert the plane if another plane is within three minutes of a potential collision. The light stays on until the approaching plane is out of range. Experts say three minutes is enough time for a plane to divert. So, if the red light is off, the plane is more than three minutes away.\n\nThe question is asking which option, if true, would most fundamentally challenge this argument. So, I need to find an option that directly contradicts the reasoning that three minutes is sufficient time to avoid collision.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nA) A near collision was averted by air traffic controllers. This shows that sometimes, even if planes are close, they can avoid collision, but it doesn't directly challenge the argument about the three-minute window. It's more about human error in some cases, not about the device's effectiveness.\n\nB) The red lights don't turn on simultaneously when two planes approach. Hmm, this might mean that one plane's light is on while another's isn't, but does that affect the argument? The argument is about the time window, not the simultaneity of alerts. So maybe not the most fundamental.\n\nC) Experts warn that three minutes might not be enough time for maneuvering without causing injuries. This directly challenges the argument because it suggests that the three-minute window isn't sufficient. If that's the case, then the device's three-minute alert isn't enough time, which would weaken the analyst's claim.\n\nD) The device only signals oncoming directions, not parallel flights. So, if planes are flying parallel, the device doesn't alert them. But the argument is about the time window, not about parallel flights. So this might not directly challenge the core point.\n\nSo, the most fundamental issue would be option C because it directly questions the sufficiency of the three-minute window, which is the basis of the argument. The other options either don't address the time window or are about other factors that don't directly impact the argument's validity.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: C\n\nThe argument hinges on the three-minute window as the critical time for avoiding collision. Option C directly challenges this by suggesting that three minutes might not be enough time for planes to maneuver safely, thus fundamentally questioning the basis of the argument.\n\nThe correct option is: C",
