anned food has more calories per ounce, but the cats eat fewer ounces. So, the total calories might be similar or even less, but the cost per day is higher. So, the only thing that's definitely supported is that the cost per day is higher, but that's not an option. Hmm.\n\nWait, maybe the answer is A because the context implies that since the total cost is higher, the per ounce cost must be higher. But that's not necessarily true because the number of ounces could be lower. For example, if canned food is $3 for 10 ounces (30 calories per ounce) and dry is $2 for 12 ounces (20 calories per ounce), the total cost per day for canned would be higher even though per ounce it's higher. Wait, in this case, per ounce, canned is $0.30 vs dry $0.17, so higher. So, in this case, A would be correct. But if the prices were different, maybe not. Wait, the context doesn't specify the prices, just that the total cost is higher. So, perhaps the per ounce cost is higher, making A correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the answer is B because the cost per day is higher for canned, but the number of ounces is lower, so the cost per day is higher despite eating fewer ounces. So, B says it costs no less, which is true because the total cost is higher. Wait, B says it costs no less to feed a cat that eats fewer ounces than one that eats more. So, if you have a cat eating fewer ounces but spending more, then feeding a cat that eats fewer ounces (canned) costs more than feeding one that eats more (dry). So, B is saying that it's no less, meaning it's equal or more. But in reality, it's more, so B is correct because it's not less. So, B is supported.\n\nWait, I'm getting confused. Let me try to structure this.\n\nThe context says:\n\n- Canned: fewer ounces per day, higher calories per ounce, higher total cost per day.\n\n- Dry: more ounces per day, lower calories per ounce, lower total cost per day.\n\nSo, the total cost per day is higher for canned, even though they eat less and have higher calories per ounce.\n\nSo, the question is which is supported.\n\nOption A: Canned costs more per ounce. The context doesn't say this directly. It only says that the total cost is higher. So, if the number of ounces is lower, the per ounce cost could be higher or lower. For example, if canned is $4 for 8 oz (so $0.50 per oz) and dry is $2 for 12 oz ($0.17 per oz), then canned is more per oz. But if canned is $2 for 8 oz ($0.25 per oz) and dry is $1 for 12 oz ($0.083 per oz), then again, canned is more per oz. Wait, in both cases, the total cost is higher for canned, and per oz is higher. So, maybe A is supported.\n\nAlternatively, if the prices were different, maybe not. But the context doesn't specify, so perhaps the only thing we can infer is that the total cost is higher, which would imply that the per ounce cost is higher because they eat less. So, A might be supported.\n\nOption B: It costs no less to feed a cat that eats fewer ounces than one that eats more. So, feeding a cat that eats fewer ounces (canned) costs more per day than feeding one that eats more (dry). So, B is saying that it's no less, which is true because it's more. So, B is supported.\n\nWait, but the question is which is most strongly supported. So, both A and B might seem supported, but let's see.\n\nThe context says that the total cost is higher for canned, even though they eat less. So, the cost per day is higher, which would mean that the cost per ounce is higher because they eat less. So, A is supported.\n\nOption B is also supported because feeding a cat that eats fewer ounces (canned) costs more per day than one that eats more (dry). So, B is also supported.\n\nWait, but the question is which is most strongly supported. Let me check the options again.\n\nOption B says that it costs no less to feed a cat that eats fewer ounces than one that eats more. So, if you have a cat eating 3 oz (canned) and another eating 5 oz (dry), the total cost per day for 3 oz is higher than for 5 oz. So, B is correct because it's more expensive to feed the one that eats fewer ounces, not less.\n\nSo, both A and B seem supported, but I think the answer is B because it's directly supported by the context, whereas A requires an assumption that the per ounce cost is higher, which might not be explicitly stated.\n\nWait, but the context says that the canned food has more calories per ounce, but the total cost is higher. So, if the calories per ounce are higher, but the number of ounces eaten is lower, the total calories might be similar or even less. So, C is not necessarily supported.\n\nSo, the correct answer is B because it's directly supported by the context that feeding a cat that eats fewer ounces (canned) costs more per day than one that eats more (dry). So, B is the correct option.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: B\n\nStep-by-step explanation:\n\nThe context states that feeding a cat canned food costs more per day than dry food, despite eating fewer ounces. This implies that the cost per day is higher for canned food, even though they consume less. Therefore, feeding a cat that eats fewer ounces (canned) costs more than one that eats more (dry), making option B correct. \n\nAnswer: B",
