t sure yet.\n\nC) A laundry detergent formulated especially for front-loading washers dissolves more readily in them than it does in top-loading washers.\n\nThis is about the dissolution rate of the special detergent. The argument doesn't provide any information about whether it dissolves more in front-loading. It just says that ordinary detergents don't dissolve well. So, C might not be necessary for the argument.\n\nD) Washers that use more water get clothes cleaner than those that use less.\n\nThis is about water usage and cleanliness. The argument is about water efficiency and detergent dissolution, not about water quantity affecting cleanliness. So, D doesn't seem relevant.\n\nSo, narrowing it down, A and B are the main contenders.\n\nOption A is stating that for clothes to be really clean, the detergent must dissolve. The argument assumes that without dissolution, the clothes won't be clean. So, if A is true, then the argument holds. If A were false, maybe a detergent could be clean without dissolving, which would weaken the argument.\n\nOption B is saying that only the special detergent can make the machine clean. The argument does suggest that a special detergent is needed, but it doesn't say that no other detergent can work. So, B might not be necessary because the argument doesn't claim that no other detergent can be used, just that a special one is needed.\n\nTherefore, the assumption required is likely A because it's a necessary link between detergent dissolution and cleanliness. Without A, the argument's conclusion wouldn't follow.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: A\n\nThe argument states that because front-loading washers use less water, ordinary detergents don't dissolve well, necessitating a special detergent. For the argument to hold, it must assume that a detergent must dissolve to be effective. If A were false, the argument's conclusion wouldn't necessarily follow.\n\nThe correct option is: A",
