      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I have this logic problem here, and I need to figure out which option correctly shows where Zhao Ming, Qian Hong, and Sun Jie were admitted. Let me try to break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, the context says that each of the three students\u2014Zhao Ming, Qian Hong, and Sun Jie\u2014was admitted to one of three universities: Peking University, Tsinghua University, and Beijing Normal University. Each of their classmates made guesses about where each person was admitted. The key point is that each classmate's guesses were half right. That means for each guess, one part was correct and the other was wrong.\n\nLet me list out the guesses:\n\n- Classmate A guessed:\n  - Zhao Ming was admitted by Tsinghua University.\n  - Sun Jie was admitted by Beijing Normal University.\n\n- Classmate B guessed:\n  - Zhao Ming was admitted by Beijing Normal University.\n  - Qian Hong was admitted by Tsinghua University.\n\n- Classmate C guessed:\n  - Zhao Ming was admitted to Peking University.\n  - Sun Jie was admitted to Tsinghua University.\n\nSince each classmate's guesses are half right, each of their two statements must have exactly one correct and one incorrect.\n\nLet me start by analyzing each classmate's guesses and see if I can find overlaps or contradictions.\n\nStarting with Classmate A:\n- A1: Zhao Ming \u2192 Tsinghua (maybe correct)\n- A2: Sun Jie \u2192 Beijing Normal (maybe correct)\n\nBut since only one is correct, either A1 is correct and A2 is wrong, or A1 is wrong and A2 is correct.\n\nSimilarly for Classmate B:\n- B1: Zhao Ming \u2192 Beijing Normal (maybe correct)\n- B2: Qian Hong \u2192 Tsinghua (maybe correct)\n\nAgain, only one is correct.\n\nClassmate C:\n- C1: Zhao Ming \u2192 Peking (maybe correct)\n- C2: Sun Jie \u2192 Tsinghua (maybe correct)\n\nOnly one correct.\n\nNow, let's see if we can find contradictions or confirmations.\n\nFirst, let's consider where Zhao Ming is admitted. He's guessed by all three classmates:\