      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out which option weakens the government's argument. The context says the government wants to charge a fee when appliances are first sold, but they're considering instead charging when they're salvaged because that would reduce waste more effectively. However, consumers might keep old appliances longer if there's a fee for discarding them.\n\nThe question is asking which option weakens this argument. Let's break down each option.\n\nOption A: Many nonfunctioning appliances can be repaired cheaply. Hmm, if these can be repaired, then maybe there's less need to salvage them, which could reduce the effectiveness of the salvage fee. But I'm not sure if this directly affects the argument about charging at first sale versus salvage.\n\nOption B: The fee gives manufacturers no incentive to make more durable appliances. If manufacturers aren't motivated to make better ones, then the argument that a salvage fee would be more effective because people keep appliances longer might hold. But this seems to support the government's position, so it doesn't weaken it.\n\nOption C: Higher disposal cost increases the incentive to dispose improperly. Wait, if it's more expensive to dispose properly, people might be more inclined to dispose improperly, which could lead to more waste. But the government's argument is about reducing waste by encouraging salvage. If people are less likely to discard properly, that's bad for their plan. So this seems to weaken the argument because it suggests that increasing the fee might not lead to more salvage but more improper disposal.\n\nOption D: For new buyers, the fee isn't paid for a few years. That means the initial push to salvage might not happen right away. But over time, as the fee is imposed, it might still encourage salvage. I'm not sure if this weakens the argument enough.\n\nSo, putting it together, Option C seems to directly oppose the government's goal of reducing waste through salvage. It suggests that making disposal more expensive might lead to more improper disposal, which would undermine the effectiveness of the salvage fee.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: C\n\nStep-by-step explanation:\n\nThe government's argument is that charging a fee at the time of salvage would reduce waste more effectively than charging at first sale because consumers would be incentivized to dispose of their old appliances sooner. However, option C suggests that increasing the disposal cost would make people more likely to dispose of appliances improperly, which would counteract the government's goal of reducing waste through salvage. This directly weakens the argument by showing that higher disposal costs could lead to more waste rather than less.\n\nAnswer: C",
