eep is not viable, which is the opposite of what the argument assumes. The argument suggests farmers should switch, so it assumes that other methods are possible.\n\nWait, maybe the correct answer is A because the argument is based on the premise that deep tillage is bad, so the assumption is that the premise is true. But no, the question is about the assumption the argument depends on, not the premises.\n\nWait, perhaps the argument assumes that farmers can change their method, so the assumption is that other methods exist. But none of the options say that. So maybe the correct answer is A because it's the only premise, but I'm not sure.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the argument assumes that farmers are aware of the problem, but that's not in the options.\n\nWait, I'm getting confused. Let me try to break it down.\n\nThe argument is: Deep tillage is bad (because it causes more erosion than no-till), so farmers should switch to other methods.\n\nThe argument's conclusion is that farmers should stop deep tillage and use other methods. So the argument assumes that farmers can or should change their method. But the options don't have that.\n\nWait, looking at the options again:\n\nA) States that deep tillage is bad because of deeper till. That's a premise.\n\nB) Says that topsoil erosion doesn't make farmers want to till more. So if erosion were making them till more, the argument's point might not hold. So the argument assumes that the reason for deep tillage isn't the erosion, but perhaps other reasons. So B could be the assumption.\n\nC) Talks about expensive methods, irrelevant.\n\nD) Talks about other methods not being viable, which is the opposite of what the argument assumes.\n\nSo the correct answer is B because the argument assumes that the farmers are not tilling deeply because of erosion, so they can switch to other methods.\n</think>\n\nThe argument assumes that farmers are not tilling deeply because of topsoil erosion, so they can switch to other methods. Therefore, the correct answer is B.\n\nThe correct option is: B",
