      "index": 0,
      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out this logic problem. Let me read it again carefully.\n\nThe context says: If Juan went to the party, it's highly unlikely that Maria would have enjoyed it. But in fact, Maria did enjoy the party, so it's highly unlikely that Juan was there.\n\nSo, the argument is that because Maria enjoyed the party, Juan probably wasn't there. It's like saying if A then not B, and since B is true, A must be false. That sounds like modus tollens.\n\nNow, the question is asking which of the options A to D has a similar pattern of reasoning. I need to analyze each option to see which one uses the same logical structure.\n\nOption A: This year's election will probably be fair. But Popov probably won't win unless the election is unfair. So, Popov won't win.\n\nBreaking this down: If the election is fair, Popov won't win (unless it's unfair). The argument concludes Popov won't win. Hmm, this seems a bit different. It's more about the condition for Popov winning, not directly using modus tollens.\n\nOption B: Kapinski is probably friendly because he sells insurance, and most people who sell insurance are friendly.\n\nThis is an argument by analogy. It's saying Kapinski is like others who sell insurance, so he's probably friendly. Not a modus tollens structure.\n\nOption C: If Clarissa missed the bus, she probably didn't get to work on time. She was at work, so it's unlikely she missed the bus.\n\nThis sounds like modus tollens. If A then not B, and B is true, so not A. Here, A is \"missed the bus,\" B is \"got to work on time.\" So, if she was on time, she didn't miss the bus. That matches the structure.\n\nOption D: If the lottery were fair, the winner wouldn't be likely to win. Since the winner would have been likely to win if it were unfair, the lottery was probably unfair.\n\nThis seems a bit confusing. It's talking about the consequences of the lottery being fair or unfair. It's more about the probability of winning based on fairness, not directly using modus tollens.\n\nSo, comparing all options, Option C uses the same structure as the original argument. It's using modus tollens: If A, then not B. Not A, therefore B. Wait, no, actually, in the original, it's If Juan went, then not Maria. Maria did, so not Juan. So, it's If A, then not B. B, therefore not A. That's modus tollens.\n\nIn Option C: If A (missed bus), then not B (on time). B (on time), so not A (didn't miss bus). That's exactly modus tollens.\n\nSo, the correct answer should be C.\n</think>\n\nThe argument presented uses modus tollens, where the conclusion is drawn by denying the consequent to infer the denial of the antecedent. Option C mirrors this structure by stating that if Clarissa missed the bus, she wouldn't have been on time, and since she was on time, she didn't miss the bus.\n\nThe correct option is: C",
