      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out which option is refuted by the given text. The context says, \"The scene of mountains is beautiful after the rain, even if no one appreciates it.\" Hmm, that's an interesting statement. Let me break it down.\n\nFirst, the text describes a beautiful scene\u2014mountains after rain. But it adds a twist: even though the scene is beautiful, no one appreciates it. So, the beauty is there, but people don't notice or value it. That makes me think about subjective appreciation and the nature of beauty.\n\nNow, looking at the options:\n\nA) Beauty is only skin deep.\nB) The taste is uncountable.\nC) Beauty is only in the eyes of people.\nD) People just want to see what they like.\n\nI need to see which of these is refuted by the text. The text is saying that beauty exists (the mountains after rain) but that people don't appreciate it. So, it's challenging the idea that beauty is only in the eyes of the beholder or that it's subjective.\n\nOption A says beauty is only skin deep. I'm not sure what that means exactly, but maybe it's implying that beauty is not real or temporary. The text doesn't directly address this, though. It's more about people not appreciating the beauty, not necessarily its depth.\n\nOption B is about taste being uncountable. That doesn't seem related to the context at all. The context is about mountains and beauty, not about taste or food. So this might not be the right answer.\n\nOption C says beauty is only in the eyes of people. The text seems to contradict this because it presents a beautiful scene that people don't appreciate. So, if beauty is only in people's eyes, then the scene wouldn't be beautiful if no one appreciates it. But the text says the scene is beautiful regardless of appreciation, so this option is refuted.\n\nOption D says people just want to see what they like. The text doesn't mention anything about wanting to see something; it's more about not appreciating it. So this doesn't seem to be refuted either.\n\nWait, but let me think again. The text is saying that beauty exists but isn't noticed. So, it's challenging the idea that beauty is only in the eyes of people because it shows that beauty can exist without appreciation. Therefore, option C is refuted because it suggests beauty is solely subjective, but the text presents an objective beauty that isn't appreciated.\n\nSo, the answer should be option C.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: C\n\nStep-by-step explanation:\n1. The text describes a beautiful mountain scene after rain but states that no one appreciates it.\n2. This challenges the idea that beauty is solely subjective (only in people's eyes).\n3. Option C suggests beauty is only in people's eyes, which is refuted by the text showing beauty exists without appreciation.\n4. Other options are unrelated or not supported by the context.\n\nThe correct option is: C",
