      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out this logic problem. Let me read it again carefully.\n\nThe context says that a recent study of prehistoric hominid skeletons found that the fractures are similar to those of rodeo riders. Therefore, the argument is that these cave dwellers were probably chasing and tackling animals, just like rodeo riders.\n\nThe question is asking which principle, if valid, justifies this argument. The options are A, B, C, D.\n\nHmm, I remember that in logic, when two things have similar effects, we can infer that their causes might be similar too. That sounds like the principle of analogy or the common cause. Let me think about each option.\n\nOption A says the most important aspect of prehistoric life is food obtaining. But the argument here is about activities, not food. So A doesn't seem relevant.\n\nOption B mentions that skeletal remains are primary clues. Well, the study does focus on skeletons, so B is relevant, but the question is about the justification for the argument, not the source of clues.\n\nOption C talks about using direct evidence over indirect. The argument uses the similarity of fractures as direct evidence to infer the activities. But the question is about the justification for using that similarity, not whether to use direct evidence.\n\nOption D says if two effects are similar, their causes are probably similar. That fits perfectly because the argument is drawing a parallel between the fractures in hominids and those in rodeo riders, concluding that their activities are similar. So D is the principle that justifies the argument.\n\nWait, let me make sure. The argument is using the fact that the effects (fractures) are similar to infer that the causes (activities) are similar. That's exactly what D is stating. So yes, D is the correct principle.\n</think>\n\nThe correct option is: D\n\nThe argument uses the similarity in skeletal fractures between prehistoric hominids and rodeo riders to infer that their activities were similar. This is justified by the principle that if two effects are similar, their causes are likely similar. \n\nAnswer: D",
