ny, the total could still go up. But the argument assumes that the new companies this year will create more jobs than last year's, or at least that their average is high enough.\n\nD) The number of jobs created last year was much larger than jobs lost. This is about net jobs, but the argument is about creating a new record, which is about total jobs added, not net. So maybe not directly relevant.\n\nWait, the argument is about creating a record, so it's about total jobs added, not net. So the assumption is that the new companies this year will add enough jobs to offset the decrease from established companies. So if new companies don't add more jobs per company than last year, then even if there are more new companies, the total might not increase.\n\nSo option A is about the jobs per company, which is a key assumption. If new companies this year don't create as many jobs per company as last year, then the total jobs might not increase enough, even if more companies start up.\n\nOption C says that new companies create more jobs on average than established companies. But the argument is about this year's new companies compared to last year's new companies. So if last year's new companies created more jobs on average, and this year's new companies don't, then the total might not increase.\n\nWait, the argument is that unless a record number of new companies start up, the total won't reach a record. So the argument assumes that the new companies this year will create a significant number of jobs, possibly more than last year's new companies. Or at least that their contribution is enough.\n\nSo the key assumption is that the number of jobs created by new companies this year is substantial enough, which relates to the jobs per company. So option A is about that.\n\nOption C is about new companies in general creating more jobs than established, but the argument is about this year's new companies compared to last year's. So maybe C isn't the right assumption.\n\nSo I think 